## Context

Coding agents are the one confirmed reader of llms.txt. Across nearly 300,000 domains the file shows no measurable effect on answer-engine citations, almost every published copy goes unrequested, and the requests that do arrive come mostly from coding agents such as Claude Code. aidemd.dev's audience below the human layer is exactly those agents: the ones an operator points at a codebase that ships intent specs. For them the site is a reference to read, not a page to rank, so this module serves reading.

Two documents serve two moments. The short index answers "what is this and where is everything" in one fetch an agent parses in milliseconds. The full text answers "give me the content" for an agent with room for the whole reference, the way large docs sites pair a compact index with a full-corpus export. The convention defines the index loosely — a required name heading, an optional summary, optional prose, then linked sections — and leaves the full-text companion as community practice, so both documents keep to the shape agent tooling already expects rather than inventing one.

The module sits inside the pre-deploy build step, which gathers the page list and each page's text, then writes and checks the files. Composing the two documents is the only work done here.

## Strategy

**Compose from what is handed in, and nothing else.** Both documents are built only from the page list the pre-deploy step passes in: each page's canonical URL, title, description, kind and text. The module reads no registry, roster or file and writes nothing. The page list is the site's one roster of indexable pages, so taking it whole means a page added, renamed or removed reaches both documents on the next build; any list kept here would drift from it.

**The index follows the llmstxt.org shape and stays short.** A product-name heading, a one-sentence summary naming the methodology and the package together, then three bare paragraphs — what AIDE, the intent-driven methodology, is, why it governs the reading agent's work in any codebase that ships intent specs, and how to start — then the linked sections. No heading sits above the first section, because spec-aware parsers expect exactly that shape, and a long preamble delays the map a coding agent came for.

**Sections follow the site's own order: docs sections, then Learn, then the site's tools.** One section per docs section in docs order, each opening with its section index; then Learn, the /learn index first and its pages in the roster's priority order; then a closing section for /observer and /brain. Separate sections keep methodology, commands, agents, skills, tools and hooks distinguishable at a glance, and taking order from the page list instead of sorting here keeps the index agreeing with the sidebar.

**Every link is the page's canonical HTML address, trailing slash included.** Links are absolute and match the canonical URL exactly, because the site serves each page only at its trailing-slash address and anything else redirects or 404s. They point at the HTML page, never its markdown copy: the page is what an agent cites back to its user, and the full text already carries the words, so a second link per page would only double the index.

**Each link carries the page's own title and description.** The link text is the title the page itself shows and the note after it is the page's own description, so the index names every page the way the page, its search result and its share card do. A page with no description gets a bare link rather than a note written here, because a description composed in this module would be a second copy of a page's words.

**The full text carries every docs and /learn page whole, in index order.** Each page appears under its own title and canonical URL, followed by the same complete text its markdown copy carries, so an agent reads the whole reference in one fetch and can cite any part back to a live page. /observer and /brain stay out: they are tools to open, not reference to read. A page handed in without its text stops composition, because a gap reads as complete.

**The fixed prose is the only authored text, and it holds the site's voice.** The heading, summary and three preamble paragraphs are the module's one piece of hand-written copy, and they name no page, so pages change without touching them. Every sentence states a fact about the methodology, the package or how to start, AIDE always appears paired with 'intent-driven' or 'aidemd', and no hype or abandoned mascot name appears, because the operator reading over the agent's shoulder is a skeptical technical lead.

## Good examples

An index whose sections follow docs order, then Learn, then the site's tools, every link canonical with its trailing slash (abridged; titles and descriptions come from each page's own record):

```
# AIDE — Autonomous Intent-Driven Engineering

> The canonical home of the AIDE methodology and the @aidemd-mcp/server package — a short `.aide` intent spec lives next to the code it governs, and that spec is the contract every agent plans, builds, and QAs against.

[three short paragraphs: what AIDE is, why it governs this agent's work, how to start]

## Methodology

- [<section index title>](https://aidemd.dev/docs/methodology/): <its description>
- [<title>](https://aidemd.dev/docs/methodology/aide-spec/): <its description>

## Commands

- [<section index title>](https://aidemd.dev/docs/commands/): <its description>
- [<title>](https://aidemd.dev/docs/commands/aide/align/): <its description>

[… Agents, Skills, Tools, Hooks in the same form …]

## Learn

- [<title>](https://aidemd.dev/learn/): <its description>
- [<title>](https://aidemd.dev/learn/intent-driven-engineering/): <its description>
- [<title>](https://aidemd.dev/learn/spec-driven-vs-intent-driven-development/): <its description>

## <closing section>

- [<title>](https://aidemd.dev/observer/): <its description>
- [<title>](https://aidemd.dev/brain/): <its description>
```

A full-text entry: the page's own title, its canonical URL, then its complete text exactly as its markdown copy carries it, followed by the next page in index order:

```
# <title of aide-spec>
Source: https://aidemd.dev/docs/methodology/aide-spec/

<the doc's complete text, frontmatter stripped, every link absolute>

# <title of the next page in index order>
Source: https://aidemd.dev/docs/methodology/aide-template/
```

## Bad examples

A composer that carries its own Learn list. A seventh /learn page goes live and is missing from both documents, and a page renamed in its record keeps its old title here:

```
## Learn

- [<title typed into the composer>](https://aidemd.dev/learn/intent-driven-engineering/)
- [<title typed into the composer>](https://aidemd.dev/learn/spec-kit-alternatives/)
```

Links that parse but mislead — no trailing slash, the markdown copy instead of the page, a relative path an agent cannot resolve:

```
- [<title>](https://aidemd.dev/docs/methodology/aide-spec): <its description>
- [<title>](https://aidemd.dev/docs/methodology/aide-spec.md): <its description>
- [<title>](/observer/): <its description>
```

A full text that shortens a page. The entry looks complete, and an agent working from it never learns the rest of the spec exists:

```
# <title of aide-spec>
Source: https://aidemd.dev/docs/methodology/aide-spec/

<the page's description, standing in for its text>
```

## References

- research/llms-txt/llms-txt -- the llmstxt.org shape (required name heading, summary, heading-free prose, linked sections with a note per link) and the full-text companion as a whole-corpus export for agents with large context, paired with a lean index on large docs sites.
- research/seo-aeo/2026-11-25-answer-engine-citation-signals-for-docs-sites -- llms.txt shows no citation effect across about 300,000 domains, 97% of files go unrequested, and coding agents are the one confirmed reader; grounds the Context framing.
- User decisions relayed by the orchestrator, 2026-11-26 and 2026-11-27 -- trailing-slash links; /learn, /observer and /brain in the index; a full-text llms-full.txt; links to the canonical HTML page rather than the markdown copy; this module composes and the pre-deploy step writes.
- .aide/intent.aide -- one page list and one true date per page feed every machine-read surface; the llms files ship for coding agents, not as a citation lever; the site's voice and the AIDE pairing rule.
- src/app/_shared/listSitePages/.aide -- the page list: every indexable page once, each with its trailing-slash canonical URL, own title, description and kind.
- src/service/docsRegistry/.aide -- docs section order, each section's index first, and each doc's text with frontmatter stripped and links absolute.
- src/data/learn/.aide -- the /learn pages' priority order and the rule that a page's title reads the same on every surface.
- scripts/publishSiteFiles/.aide -- the pre-deploy step that hands in the page list, writes both files and fails the build on a broken one.
